Resource

A transfer of more data than one packet holds, over an established
link. Seven context bytes, six of them link data packets and one a
proof. Start at doc/link for the link itself and at doc/fields for the
field names.


Constants

    MAPHASH_LEN         4   bytes     RNS/Resource.py#MAPHASH_LEN
    RANDOM_HASH_SIZE    4   bytes     RNS/Resource.py#RANDOM_HASH_SIZE
    SDU                 464 bytes     RNS/Resource.py#SDU
    HASHMAP_MAX_LEN     74  entries   RNS/Resource.py#HASHMAP_MAX_LEN
    WINDOW              4   parts     RNS/Resource.py#WINDOW
    MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE  1048575 bytes RNS/Resource.py#MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE

HASHMAP_MAX_LEN is derived from the link MDU and is how many four-byte
map hashes fit in one advertisement or one update. It decides where the
hashmap of a transfer needs a second packet, and nothing on the wire
states it.

MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE decides something else: where the transfer needs a
second segment. See "Segments" below.


Contexts

    0x01   resource        one part
    0x02   resource_adv    an advertisement, msgpack
    0x03   resource_req    a part request
    0x04   resource_hmu    a hashmap update, msgpack
    0x05   resource_prf    a proof, and a packet of type proof
    0x06   resource_icl    the sender cancels
    0x07   resource_rcl    the receiver cancels

    RNS/Packet.py#RESOURCE


One token, cut into parts

The data is encrypted once, through the link, and the resulting token
is then cut into parts of SDU bytes:

    self.data = self.link.encrypt(self.data)

    RNS/Resource.py#link.encrypt

So a part is a slice of one token and not a token of its own. Only the
first part begins with the initialisation vector and only the last ends
with the hmac, and no part can be opened alone. A decoder that reads a
part as a token finds 16 bytes it takes for an iv and no hmac that
verifies.

The packet layer does not encrypt a part either, for the same reason it
does not encrypt a keepalive: the payload is already ciphertext.

    RNS/Packet.py#RESOURCE

A part is named by its map hash, four bytes:

    map_hash = SHA-256(part || random_hash)[0:4]

    RNS/Resource.py#get_map_hash

The random hash is the resource's, from the advertisement, so a part
packet does not carry enough to name itself. That is not an oversight:
the receiver matches parts against a hashmap it already has, and a part
that arrives without one belongs to no transfer it is expecting. No
vector records a map hash for that reason.

Four bytes is short enough to collide, and the reference says so: it
keeps a guard list while cutting and remaps the whole resource if two
parts hash alike.

    RNS/Resource.py#collision_guard_list


Advertisement, 0x02

A msgpack map of eleven one-character keys, in this order:

    t   uint   transfer size, the encrypted length
    d   uint   data size, before compression and encryption
    n   uint   number of parts
    h   bin    resource hash, 32 bytes
    r   bin    random hash, 4 bytes
    o   bin    original hash, 32 bytes; the first segment's
    i   uint   segment index
    l   uint   total segments
    q   bin    request id, or nil
    f   uint   flags, one byte
    m   bin    hashmap, four bytes per part

    RNS/Resource.py#dictionary

The order is the order the reference writes them and msgpack preserves
it, but a map is not a sequence: a reader that goes by position rather
than by key agrees with this reference and with nothing that is
entitled to reorder them. unpack names all eleven and names them in
another order than pack writes them, so neither the order nor the count
is a rule of the format.

    RNS/Resource.py#unpack

test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-reordered is that map: the same
eleven keys and the same values, written by the reference's own
umsgpack in the order unpack reads them. It is the same length as the
advertisement it was built from and not the same bytes, and the
reference reads the two identically.

What follows from reading by key is that a key which is not there costs
its own field and nothing else, and that a key which is there and is
none of the eleven costs nothing at all. The vectors below carry the
cases, all of them plaintexts the layers above the packet never build:

    resource-advertisement-no-hash        h is absent; the other ten
                                          fields all read
    resource-advertisement-not-a-number   t is a string; the ten keys
                                          after it still read
    resource-advertisement-not-a-map      one byte, no map at all;
                                          eleven dashes
    resource-advertisement-extra-key      a twelfth key, second in the
                                          map; the eleven still read
    resource-advertisement-foreign-types  five pairs of no field, of
                                          every type; the eleven read
    resource-advertisement-ext-types      six pairs of no field, of the
                                          ext widths; the eleven read
    resource-advertisement-float32        one pair of no field, holding
                                          a float of four bytes
    resource-advertisement-bin16          one pair of no field, holding
                                          256 bytes
    resource-advertisement-str16          the same 256 bytes, as a
                                          string
    resource-advertisement-ext16          the same 256 bytes, as an ext
    resource-advertisement-bin32          a length of 65536 with no
                                          body behind it
    resource-advertisement-str32          the same length under the
                                          string head byte
    resource-advertisement-ext32          the same length, and a type
                                          byte it does not count
    resource-advertisement-array32        65536 elements announced and
                                          none held
    resource-advertisement-map32          65536 pairs announced and
                                          none held

Every vector from the twelfth key down is the direction a decoder is
likeliest to get wrong, because nothing the reference writes today
exercises it. The twelfth
key is two bytes where every name in the format is one, and its value
is a float64 where the format holds only uints and bins, so a reader
that expects the shape it has seen rather than the shape msgpack
declares stops at it and loses the ten keys behind it. The reference
does not read the map at all until umsgpack has turned it into a
dictionary, and then asks that dictionary for eleven names.

The fifth vector is that read to its end. Its five foreign pairs hold
between them every type umsgpack can write into a packet and this
format does not otherwise use: both signs of fixint, the four signed
widths, uint64, both booleans, a string too long for fixstr, an array
and a map at both of the widths a packet can reach, and one pair named
by a number rather than by a string. Each is stepped over by its head
byte alone, because a value with no field here is measured and not
read.

The sixth vector holds what the fifth leaves out. An ext value is a bin
value with a type byte in front of its body, and umsgpack writes one for
any Ext a caller packs; no handler is registered here or in the
reference. Six pairs carry the widths a packet can reach: the five
fixext widths, whose length is a power of two read out of the head byte
and not the low bits of it, and ext8, which writes a length where a
fixext writes none. The type byte is outside that length in every one of
the six, and it is the byte a reader that measures an ext as a bin is
short by. Such a reader lands one byte inside the pair after it and
loses the eleven.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack_ext

The seventh holds a width neither of them reaches. A float of four
bytes is what packb writes for a caller that asks it for single
precision, and asking is not replacing: the option is packb's own
argument and every byte of the map is still umsgpack's. An
advertisement holds no float anywhere, so a reader that has met one has
met it in a pair like this one.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack_float

The eighth is the first of the two-byte length class, which packb
reaches only for a value of more than 255 bytes. One such value beside
the eleven keys is 388 bytes where a packet holds 431, so a width in
this class fits in no vector that already carries another and each has
a packet to itself. A large resource writes a bin16 for its own
hashmap, which is the same head byte read as a field rather than
stepped over; the two are different code and a decoder can hold one and
not the other.

The ninth is str16, the same measurement under another head byte. A
string and a binary differ in what their bytes mean and not in how far
they reach, and the two vectors are the same length, so a decoder that
holds one head byte and not the other passes the eighth and fails this.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack_string

The tenth is ext16, which closes the class and puts the ext rule and
the two-byte length in one value. Its two bytes count the body and not
the type byte, so a reader that takes 256 from them and moves 256 stops
one byte short of the pair after it, where the eighth and ninth stop
exactly on it. It is one byte longer than either.

The eleventh opens the four-byte class, which packb reaches only above
65535 bytes or elements. No packet holds a value that long, so what a
packet holds of one is its header, and the vector is packb's own output
for 65536 bytes with every byte of the body cut off. The length is
whole and says 65536; nought bytes follow it. The map is cut, so
nothing in it reads and every field is a dash.

That is the same eleven dashes a reader which has never met the head
byte prints, and the vector separates neither of those readers from the
other. What it separates is both of them from the reader that adds
65536 to a pointer before it compares the number to what is left.
resource-advertisement-truncated stops one byte earlier than this one,
before a length rather than after one, and the two bugs are not the
same bug.

The twelfth is str32, and it is the eleventh under another head byte.
bin16 and str16 are the same length as each other because their bodies
are; these two are the same length because neither has a body, so the
head byte is the one byte in the two plaintexts that differs.

The thirteenth closes the class. An ext32 header is six bytes where the
two above are five: the type byte sits behind the four-byte length and
outside it, so the cut keeps a byte the length does not count and this
plaintext is one byte longer than either of theirs. That is the same
byte ext16 is longer than bin16 and str16 by, measured where there is
no body for it to be confused with.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack_ext

The fourteenth is array32, where the four bytes count elements and not
bytes. The three above are stepped over by adding a length to a
pointer; this one is stepped over by reading 65536 values, and it holds
none of them. A reader that takes the count for a byte count moves
65536 bytes it does not have, and a reader that reads it as elements
runs out on the first. Both end in eleven dashes, which is why this
vector is not the three above under a fourth head byte.

An array is where the dash in a request body means the element was not
reached and the elements before it stand. That rule does not reach
inside a map: a pair whose value is an array the plaintext ran out of
is a pair umsgpack never finished, so the map was never built and the
eleven do not read. tools/gen read it the other way until this vector,
and reported the eleven where cmd/dump reported dashes.

The fifteenth closes the class. A map32 counts pairs, so the same four
bytes announce twice the values array32 announces, and this one holds
none of them either. It is a map inside a map, which is where an inner
map that is nothing has to be told from an inner map holding a nil: the
first takes the outer map with it and the second is a value like any
other. tools/gen had the same gap here it had at array32, and closing
it is why a map that is nothing is no longer read as a nil anywhere.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack_map

With that, every type msgpack defines has a vector here. The one head
byte that has none is 0xc1, which the format reserves and no packer
writes.

The fifth vector is also where the map's own header stops being a
fixmap. 16 pairs is where the count leaves the head byte, and eleven
keys with five pairs beside them is the first map16 an advertisement
reaches. A reader that takes the pair count from the low four bits of
the head byte finds five pairs where there are 16, and loses the eleven.
The sixth is a map16 too, at 17 pairs.

Two more are read by neither rule, because they are not read at all. A
map is whole or it is nothing, which is where it parts company with an
array body on a link: umsgpack builds the dictionary or it raises, and
unpack looks its eleven names up in what was built.

    resource-advertisement-truncated      cut inside a length prefix;
                                          eleven dashes
    resource-advertisement-duplicate-key  one name on two pairs;
                                          eleven dashes

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#InsufficientDataException,
    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#DuplicateKeyException

The truncated plaintext is the reference's own advertisement, cut after
the head byte of the resource hash and before the byte that says how
long that hash is. It is the only vector in the corpus that stops
inside a value rather than between two of them, which is where a reader
that takes a length it has not read yet reads past its buffer. Three
keys are whole ahead of the cut and none of the three reads: the map
they are in was never built.

The other is the same advertisement with the name of its second key
altered to the name of its first. Eleven pairs, eleven values and ten
names, all of them whole, and no field reads either. A reader that
fills its fields as it scans the pairs keeps one of the two values and
reports ten fields the reference never produced; which of the two it
keeps is not the point.

Both are why the dash in an advertisement is read as one thing and not
two. It does not say that a field was absent or malformed. It says the
map holds no answer for that name, and eleven of them say the map was
never a map.

A uint is written at the width the value needs and a bin at the width
its length needs, so the widths in an advertisement are decided by the
size of the resource. Every one the format can reach is in
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-large, a resource of 100000
bytes:

    t, d      uint32, 0xce, above 65535
    n         uint8,  0xcc, 216 parts
    m         bin16,  0xc5, a hashmap of 296 bytes

Every other advertisement here holds those as a fixint, a uint16 and a
bin8. A decoder that reads the width it saw last, or that implements
one branch per key, passes them and fails on the first resource that
is not small.

    RNS/vendor/umsgpack.py#_pack2

The resource hash is over the data and the random hash together, so two
transfers of identical data are two resources:

    hash = SHA-256(data || random_hash)

    RNS/Resource.py#random_hash

The flags byte is six bits, low to high:

    0   encrypted
    1   compressed
    2   split
    3   is request
    4   is response
    5   has metadata

    RNS/Resource.py#self.f

Only the byte is recorded. Four of the six bits have a vector, and each
comes with an object that sets it rather than from a table of
permutations; see doc/packet. Bit 0 is set in every transfer here,
because a resource on a link is encrypted. Bit 5 is set in
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-metadata, whose resource was
constructed with metadata: the map is the same 11 keys and nothing but
this byte says the metadata is there. Bit 2 is set in
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-split, and what it is for is the
section below.

Bit 1 is set in test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-compressed. The
reference compresses with bz2 before it encrypts, keeps the result
where it came out smaller, and says so in this bit alone: t counts the
compressed bytes and d the bytes the receiver ends up with, and neither
field states which of the two happened. That vector and
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement carry the same 1800 bytes and
differ in one argument to the constructor, so t falls from 1856 to 112
and the part count from 4 to 1.

    RNS/Resource.py#compressed_data

t below d is therefore not a mark of compression. It is also what a
segment of a split resource writes, where t counts the segment and d
the whole; see below.

The hashmap in an advertisement holds at most HASHMAP_MAX_LEN entries,
which is 74 at this MTU. A resource with more parts than that carries
the first 74 and sends the rest in hashmap updates, context 0x04.
test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-large is such an advertisement: it
declares 216 parts and carries 74 hashes, and the count and the length
of the hashmap are the only two places that say so. It is not a split
resource: its flags byte is 0x01 and it advertises one segment.


Segments

A resource of more than MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE is cut into segments, and
each segment is advertised, transferred and proved on its own. The
threshold is a size and not a part count, so it has nothing to do with
HASHMAP_MAX_LEN above.

    total segments = ((total size - 1) // MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE) + 1

    RNS/Resource.py#total_segments

Segment 1 reads the first MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE bytes less the metadata,
every segment after it reads MAX_EFFICIENT_SIZE.

Three fields of the map carry it. i is the index, from 1. l is the
total. o is the hash of segment 1, which is the name the whole transfer
goes by, while h names this segment alone. For segment 1 the two are
equal, and o is set from h where the constructor is given no original
hash; for every segment after it they differ.

    RNS/Resource.py#original_hash

test/linkdata/resource-advertisement-split is segment 2 of 2 and the
only vector in the corpus where o and h disagree. d is the size of the
whole resource and t the encrypted length of this segment alone, so
this is also the one advertisement where t is far below d.


Part request, 0x03

Three pieces concatenated, with no count, no type byte and no
delimiter. The reader takes them by length:

    offset  size  field
    0       1     hashmap exhausted: 0xff, and 0x00 otherwise
    1       4     the last map hash, only when exhausted
    -       32    the resource hash
    -       -     four bytes per part requested

    RNS/Resource.py#request_data

The flag is one value compared against HASHMAP_IS_EXHAUSTED, not a
byte tested for truth. A sender writes only 0x00 or 0xff, and a reader
that treats every non-zero byte as exhausted looks for the last map
hash where a 0x01 has no last map hash to find.

    RNS/Resource.py#HASHMAP_IS_EXHAUSTED

The flag decides where the resource hash begins. A decoder that reads a
fixed offset is right on every request until the receiver runs out of
hashes, and then reads the resource hash four bytes early and every map
hash after it wrongly.

The receiver asks for at most WINDOW parts at a time, and the window
grows as parts arrive, so the number of map hashes in a request is not
fixed either.

Nothing in the payload states its own length, so the plaintext is the
only bound a reader has. The least a request can be is one byte, and
once the flag has been read the least it can be is

    33 bytes    the flag and the resource hash
    37 bytes    when the flag is set, with the last map hash between

A plaintext shorter than that is rejected as short-plaintext, with the
threshold beside it; see doc/fields. The packet around it decoded, so
the header, the token and the keys stand. Four vectors hold the four
ways to be too short:

    test/linkdata/resource-request-empty
    test/linkdata/resource-request-short
    test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted-short
    test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update-short


Hashmap update, 0x04

    offset  size  field
    0       32    the resource hash
    32      -     msgpack [segment index, hashmap]

    RNS/Resource.py#hmu

Sent by the sender when a part request says the receiver has run out of
hashes. The hash is raw and what follows it is msgpack, which is the
one payload in the corpus that is half of each.

The msgpack half carries its own lengths and a reader cannot run past
them. The raw half carries none, so the least an update can be is 33
bytes: the hash and one byte of the array behind it. Shorter is
rejected as short-plaintext.

That one byte does not have to be an array header, and a byte that is
not one is no rejection either. The rule is the one a request body
follows: the elements it would have yielded are "-", the hash before it
is fixed width and reads either way.
test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update-not-an-array is 33 bytes whose
last is 0x01. See doc/link.


Proof, 0x05

A packet of type proof, not data, and not encrypted, addressed to the
link id. Its payload is 64 bytes:

    offset  size  field
    0       32    the resource hash
    32      32    SHA-256(data || resource_hash)

    RNS/Resource.py#proof_data

That is the same packet type and the same length as an implicit
delivery proof, and neither half means here what it means there: the
second 32 bytes are a hash of the data, not a signature over anything,
and nothing about the packet is signed. The context byte is the only
thing that tells the two apart, which is why the corpus files this as
its own kind, resourceproof, rather than as a form of proof.

A receiver that has the whole resource can compute it. So can anyone
else who has the data, which is what it is for: it says the transfer
arrived, not who sent it.

64 bytes is the only length that decodes. Any other is rejected
as invalid-length, with the one accepted length beside it, and
test/resourceproof/invalid-length is one byte under. A delivery proof
has two accepted lengths where this has one, and 64 is one of them: a
decoder that reads the context byte and then hands the packet to its
delivery proof path takes a hash of the data for a signature.


Cancels, 0x06 and 0x07

The resource hash isi 32 bytes. 0x06 is the sender giving up and 0x07 the
receiver. Either end sends one only while it still believes the transfer
is running: a resource that has completed cancels nothing.

    RNS/Resource.py#COMPLETE


Vector format

kind: linkdata for six of the seven, determinism: encode

raw is three lines, as for every other packet on a link:

    line 1    the link request packet
    line 2    the responder's 32-byte link private key
    line 3    the complete packet

    test/linkdata/resource-advertisement
    test/linkdata/resource-part
    test/linkdata/resource-request
    test/linkdata/resource-request-exhausted
    test/linkdata/resource-hashmap-update
    test/linkdata/resource-cancel
    test/linkdata/resource-cancel-receiver

The exhausted request and the hashmap update come from a transfer of
more parts than one hashmap segment holds; the others from a transfer
of four parts, and the two cancels from one that is stopped after the
advertisement.

kind: resourceproof, determinism: encode

raw is two lines:

    line 1    the resource hash the receiver was advertised
    line 2    the complete packet

The hash is an input because the packet names a resource and says
nothing else about it. hash_match is the comparison, and it is the only
check a resource proof admits without the data.
